Aurelio, Beatrix, Joss,

The third-quarter numbers closed on Friday and they are not where we need
them to be. I want to set out the position and what I am asking of each of
you before the Finance and Performance Committee next meets. Please treat
this as confidential to your own management teams.

1. Where we are

At the end of March the three divisions were overspent by $2.14 million in
total against the year-to-date budget:

  West Division      $0.92 million over
  Central Division   $0.41 million over
  Harbour Division   $0.81 million over

Overtime is the largest single cause. We have spent $7.9 million on overtime
in nine months against a full-year budget of $6.3 million. West accounts for
almost half of that, driven by operator vacancies at Marrow Lane. Harbour's
overspend is mostly the unplanned engine work on MV Sablewick Star in
February, which cost $540,000 and kept her out of service for 23 days.

Fare revenue is also behind. We have taken $68.4 million against a
year-to-date budget of $70.1 million, a shortfall of $1.7 million. Laszlo's
team attributes about a third of that to the wet January and the rest to
slower growth on the ferries.

On present trends the Authority would finish the year about $3.1 million
overspent. The general reserve can absorb that, but I am not prepared to go
to the Board in June asking them to adopt next year's budget on the back of
an overspend of that size without showing that we acted.

2. Controls from Monday 4 May until 30 June

(a) No new agency driver bookings. Existing bookings run to their end date
    and are not extended.

(b) Every Band C commitment needs my countersignature as well as the
    Director's approval. Send requests to Ottoline Graves in my office; she
    will turn them round within two working days.

(c) Band A and Band B spending is unchanged, but I will be looking at any
    pattern of orders just under a band limit.

(d) Recruitment of bus operators, light rail operators and ferry crew
    continues. All other vacancies are frozen unless Priyanka and I both
    agree an exception.

(e) Overtime above the weekly limit already needs Renata's approval under
    the revised policy. Renata has agreed to copy me on every approval she
    gives between now and the year end.

3. What I need from you

A recovery plan from each division by Friday 8 May. It should show, month
by month, how you will reduce the overspend by 30 June and what would have
to give. One page is enough. I will take the three plans to the next
meeting of the Finance and Performance Committee and I would like each of
you to attend for that item.

Joss - please include a note on whether the Sablewick Star repair can be
recovered in part from the engine manufacturer under warranty. Halvard's
team thinks there may be a claim.

Aurelio - I know the vacancy position at Marrow Lane is the root of your
overtime. Tell me what it would take to fill the 31 vacant operator posts
by September and I will look at it separately from the freeze.

4. One piece of better news

The diesel forward contracts have saved us $0.6 million so far this year
against the spot price, and electricity costs for the Copperline are
$0.2 million under budget.

I am in the office all week. Call me on extension 4102 if you want to talk
any of this through before you write your plan.

Desmond

